WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:05.120
Speaker 1: But you know who should be worried
right now, the Democratic Party.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:12.740
A recent report by NBC News says that by all
indicators.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:20.660
The Democrats are basically poised for a midterm
shellacking in twenty twenty two.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:25.730
Now, before we even get into any of these
indicators, let's talk about history.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:26.730
Right.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.610
And this is something that I'm sure David
talked about.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:29.610
I know.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:30.610
I've talked about it.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:36.190
And, yes, typically the party that retakes
the White House will end up losing the midterms

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:40.780
in the following election.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.329
Happen to Obama, it happened to Trump, it
happened to Clinton, it happened, you know,

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:45.329
it happens.

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:46.329
It happens, it happens.

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:50.840
Historically, you're more likely to lose that
first midterm than you are to win it.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.300
Obviously, there are a couple of exceptions
out there.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.690
But that's typically what happens.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:03.940
So even just historically, Biden and the Democrats
would be poised to lose, but this NBC News

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:09.970
report says, hold up now, folks, it's a little
bit more involved than even just that.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:14.110
First and foremost, redistricting is happening.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:19.650
And because Republicans control more state
legislatures than the Democrats do, they get

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.980
to redraw more districts than the Democrats.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.940
In fact, the number here is astounding.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:32.120
You see, it is Republicans get to redraw one
hundred and eighty seven districts.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.350
Democrats get to redraw 75.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:42.930
Yeah, that's not good, that's not good, especially
for the party that obviously has a very strong

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.370
history of gerrymandering.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:50.590
I mean, if you want to look at what a gerrymandered
district looks like, look no further further

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.460
than Dan Crenshaw's district.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.980
You know, you got your little almost a square
thing and then you got this big, long loop

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:01.021
that curves around in a sea scooping up all
the little Republican areas.

00:02:01.021 --> 00:02:03.600
It's a ridiculously gerrymandered district.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.060
And of course, with Republicans getting to
redraw more than twice as many as the Democrats,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.030
we're going to see more districts that look
like that.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.209
And that is unfortunate.

00:02:14.209 --> 00:02:19.849
And it's something that we can't do a whole
heck of a lot about right now, which I guess

00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:21.159
just got to sit and watch it happen.

00:02:21.159 --> 00:02:22.799
And that's terrifying.

00:02:22.799 --> 00:02:26.510
That's a horrible way to have to look at these
things, but that's what it is.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:31.989
Second big indicator that NBC News points
out is the money, right?

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:37.459
Statistically, the candidate that raises the
most money has the best chance of winning.

00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:40.540
And right now, Republicans are definitely
outraising Democrats.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.230
They have more cash on hand than the Democratic
Party.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.670
So that's a big advantage for them.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:55.489
Another one that's actually, I guess, happened
after NBC News had written this article or

00:02:55.489 --> 00:03:01.530
they just decided not to include it for some
reason is Afghanistan.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:06.670
Afghanistan is going to play a role in the
twenty, twenty two midterms, and because Biden

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:12.219
was president when all this chaos happened,
when the Taliban retook Afghanistan, it's

00:03:12.219 --> 00:03:17.739
going to fall at his feet and Republicans
are going to try to use that to their advantage.

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:23.390
So that is a big hurdle that the Democratic
Party is going to have to overcome.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.840
And I don't know how they do it.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.480
Pulling the troops out was the right decision.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.019
The power vacuum created because of that,
yes, it's the failures of the United States,

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:42.159
but it's the failures of 20 years, not just
the failures of six months of Joe Biden.

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:43.469
So.

00:03:43.469 --> 00:03:47.469
Unfair to put it at his feet, absolutely.

00:03:47.469 --> 00:03:51.780
But Republicans are going to try to do that
anyway, then, of course, you have covered

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:57.329
coming back, which again is not Joe Biden's
fault, but Republicans are going to try to

00:03:57.329 --> 00:03:58.329
make it that way.

00:03:58.329 --> 00:04:02.769
As as we've talked about, Donald Trump is
already trying to say that Joe Biden surrendered

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:08.969
to covid and if covid is still around by the
twenty, twenty two midterms.

00:04:08.969 --> 00:04:10.389
That's going to hurt Democrats a lot.

00:04:10.389 --> 00:04:14.389
It really, really is, especially, you know,
what's the breakdown of the House now is what?

00:04:14.389 --> 00:04:18.700
Two hundred and twenty Democrats to two hundred
and twelve Republicans?

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:23.500
Another problem Democrats have is that they've
got several Democrats in swing districts who

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:30.910
are incumbents who are not running for office
or they're seeking a Senate seat instead.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:37.300
So those districts now become toss ups instead
of having a strong Democratic incumbent.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:42.539
And what happens when you have districts like
that where an incumbent does not run, whether

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:48.770
it's a Republican or Democrat, by the way,
you have to spend more money on an unknown

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:50.979
than you would on an incumbent.

00:04:50.979 --> 00:04:57.479
So that race, even if you win, it, is going
to take more resources to be able to win it.

00:04:57.479 --> 00:05:00.490
So that's a big drain on the Democrats.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.170
So how do they fix this?

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:08.860
Obviously, the easy answer you deliver for
the American public, they got the child tax

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:11.900
credit going out right now, that's great.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:16.479
You're showing people, look, we are giving
you money, we're giving you money to help

00:05:16.479 --> 00:05:19.020
you through this pandemic.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.669
So that's a positive, the reconciliation bill,
three and a half trillion dollars, depending

00:05:23.669 --> 00:05:29.199
on whether or not sentiment mansion shave
off some of that, that includes real tangible

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:34.810
benefits for pretty much every single district
here in the United States.

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:38.300
That is a must pass piece of legislation.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:42.740
And honestly, I think the midterms are going
to hinge on what happens with that reconciliation

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.610
bill.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:49.530
If you make promises to the public, as Joe
Biden did, a promise to get rid of some student

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:55.539
loan debt, they've done a little, but not
in any kind of widespread way that affects

00:05:55.539 --> 00:05:57.550
a lot of people.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:58.669
And that's unfortunate.

00:05:58.669 --> 00:06:00.750
That could come back to bite you.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:06.949
So if you deliver big time with this reconciliation
bill and you give people something tangible,

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:12.099
something they can either hold or see for
themselves that you have done.

00:06:12.099 --> 00:06:19.879
You just might stave off what could be a midterm
bloodbath for the Democratic Party.

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:25.030
So that's where it is, you know what to do,
so get busy on it.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.740
I'm Ferren Cousins in for David Pakman.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:28.280
We'll be right back

